  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>The IRGC struck three commercial tankers on 7 July and two additional tankers on 13 July, breaking the post-MOU traffic recovery before it consolidated; CENTCOM reimposed the US naval blockade through the Strait on 13 July. IMF PortWatch AIS data recorded daily transits falling from approximately 34 vessels/day at the 5 July peak — the high point since MCM operations commenced 16 June — back to approximately 19/day by 13 July. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>Iran's retaliatory salvo on 13–14 July struck military facilities in five countries: Al Udeid Air Base (Qatar), a US-affiliated installation in Bahrain, two Kuwaiti positions, facilities in the UAE, and Israeli air-defense batteries in the Negev. The strikes drew IDF air-defense responses against the Israeli component and prompted US diplomatic notification to Gulf-state hosts. No Iranian attack crossed into a non-military civilian population center during the 13–14 July window. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Trump announced on 15 July a conditional 48-hour halt on US airstrikes in exchange for a written Iranian commitment to return to the MOU negotiating table; Netanyahu responded the same day that Israel would not halt independent air operations regardless of US posture. Qatar mediators made two additional trips between Doha, Washington, and Tehran across 12–13 July but produced no public readout confirming a named convened session through 14 July. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>CENTCOM announced the fourth consecutive night of airstrikes on Iranian military infrastructure 14–15 July, targeting IRGC ballistic missile storage and naval patrol boat facilities near Bandar Abbas. Mojtaba Khamenei issued a written directive on 15 July to IRGC commanders authorizing continued maritime operations in the Gulf in response to the US strikes. The strike tempo and the written directive together constitute the conditions under which Qatar's courier track is operating as of EOD 15 July. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>The Hormuz transit arc from pre-war through 15 July: approximately 88–110/day pre-war baseline; near-zero after the mine-laying campaign; approximately 10–15/day during the early MCM phase (late June); a peak of approximately 34/day by 5 July as coalition mine clearance produced the first commercial lane; back to approximately 19/day on 13 July following the IRGC tanker strikes and blockade reimposition. IMO and Bloomberg reporting as of 26 June estimated approximately 80 mines remaining in the primary shipping lane; the dual-route dispute — Iran requiring access through the northern Omani channel, the US insisting on the traditional southern lane — has not been resolved and is the current bottleneck to any resumption of clearance-lane traffic above 20/day. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

