  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>IRNA, Tasnim, and Fars covered the 13–14 July strike sequence under a 'decisive response' narrative that framed the IRGC five-country salvo as proportionate retaliation for four nights of US strikes on Iranian military infrastructure. Kayhan's 15 July front page led with 'IRGC Active Deterrence: The Message Has Been Sent' and carried a graphic mapping all five strike countries. No Iranian state media outlet published casualty figures from Iranian military sites hit in US airstrikes; IRIB ran an uninterrupted four-hour broadcast beginning when the July 13 tanker strike news broke. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's 15 July written directive to IRGC commanders was carried by IRNA as a formal document, not an editorial or speech excerpt — a publication format used on only three prior occasions in 2025–2026, each preceding a confirmed IRGC kinetic operation within 72 hours. Press TV's English-language output on the five-country salvo was notably muted relative to Farsi and Arabic services, a divergence pattern that recurred during the early June escalation. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

